WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.690
- I got several emails
to set up today's video.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:03.850
So the first part is:

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.670
there is a massive archive

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.280
hidden in a working salt mine
in the north of England.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.170
And the second part is:
it's not just for documents.

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.120
Because there's so much space available,

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.020
it's also an archive for actual
physical objects, for "stuff".

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.260
Now it's very rare that
someone who is not

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.770
an employee or an archive client
is allowed down here,

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:22.710
particularly to make a video.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.640
Because there are public records,
important government archives,

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.840
and a lot of things that
I'm not allowed to go near,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:28.830
let alone film.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.580
Confidentiality is important,

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.200
and someone is looking
over my camera op's shoulder

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.120
to check where that lens is pointing.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.490
We are 150 metres underground

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:38.937
and about a kilometre into
that mine...

00:00:38.937 --> 00:00:40.202
is DeepStore.

00:00:40.202 --> 00:00:41.320
Ready when you are!

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.830
- The area that the mine occupies
is around 3.5km east to west,

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:53.750
and about 2.5km north to south.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.480
And at the moment we're about
1.5km away from the mining activities.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.300
We use what's called a
"room and pillar" mining technique.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.060
We don't just extract great
big massive voids of salt.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.980
There are massive pillars of salt,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.230
of which I'm actually
stood next to one now,

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.320
which make the mine so stable
and support the roof.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.244
So where DeepStore comes in is:

00:01:13.244 --> 00:01:15.770
we actually build inbetween
these pillars,

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.430
and in-between these
pillars is what we call

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.810
the repositories,
or the rooms.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.463
And then we plane the floors,
they're painted,

00:01:21.463 --> 00:01:23.930
we install the racking,
we install the air conditioning.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:27.060
Originally, the mining started
around about 1850

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.320
and it really got going around
the turn of the century, 1900.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.800
Since then, it's simply
grown and grown and grown

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.720
up to the present day
where now we're extracting

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.700
around a million tonnes each year.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.870
DeepStore was set up in the 1990s

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.640
because we've got the
perfect atmosphere

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.070
down here to store items.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.690
The salt creates a naturally
occurring dry atmosphere.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.730
And of course with the racking,

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.750
nothing naturally comes
into contact with the salt.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.820
Because this is relatively a shallow mine,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.420
we're around 150 metres,
400-500 feet,

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.980
and because of the salt bed,

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.180
it has created this natural ambient
temperature of 14-15°

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.810
along with a relative
humidity of 53-55%,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.860
which anybody in the archive
and storage world knows,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.410
naturally occurring,
that is absolutely fantastic.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.570
What we've been able to do is actually
enhance these conditions

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.240
and modify them even more
to client requirements.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.910
So by the use of air conditioning
and air filtration,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.400
we can make the atmosphere in here
to really whatever a client requires.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.040
The atmosphere in these storage rooms

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.090
is actually clearer than
it is on the surface.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.020
- It's almost impossible to get across

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.210
the sheer scale of the mine
and the archive on camera.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.250
And out there, where it's
still a working mine,

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.780
I can taste salt in the air,
there's clouds of it that lands on you.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.950
But in here, things are a
little bit more controlled.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.300
It is public knowledge

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.440
that somewhere in the
many, many vaults here,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.810
there are files from the
British National Archives

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.960
and core samples drilled out
for soil tests

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.920
during the construction of Crossrail.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.270
Other than that, well,
DeepStore aren't saying.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.750
More through wanting to be
cautious about

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.200
revealing details of their clients,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.685
rather than any strict
secrecy.

00:03:02.685 --> 00:03:06.020
But the folks who actually got
in touch and invited me here

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.530
have a very different reason

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.610
for storing actual physical
things long-term.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:15.480
- Laura Ashley is a UK interiors lifestyle
and fashion brand.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.610
Within the cabinets in this room,
there are hand drawn artworks,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.830
some of them hundreds of years old

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.670
that have been developed
into wallpapers or fabrics.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:29.670
But we also have fabrics,
dresses, rolls of wallpaper,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:34.600
metres of the fabric that we've produced
over the years stored in the archive.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.280
It's a continually growing
archive for Laura Ashley.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.810
It's really important for us to archive
the original physical pieces.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.240
So we can really understand,

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.400
it's those techniques of
how they were made.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.800
If you scanned that, if you
created a digital archive,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.630
you would lose that history to that piece.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.090
When you can touch and feel

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.570
and see some of these
amazing antique documents,

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.110
you can really see the
brushstrokes in the artwork

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.680
or how they were printed initially.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.930
And it might be that we
actually want to recreate

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:08.360
how they were printed,
not just how they look.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.690
- We can't keep everything.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.910
It would be nice if entropy didn't exist,

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.830
but until humanity fixes that,

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:18.420
preservation and archiving will
take skill, time, and money.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.350
And the more you preserve,
the more upkeep it takes.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.220
Perhaps the most important decision
any archivist has to make

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.320
is what to keep and
what to throw away.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.410
- So we've got almost a
hundred thousand items

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.080
in this Laura Ashley archive,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.880
but that is by no means everything
that was ever manufactured.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.010
We will always keep masters

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.470
so wallpaper and fabric is
core to what Laura Ashley is.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.640
Original artworks would
never be thrown away.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:43.620
The Laura Ashley pieces

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.680
in this archive date back
to the really early period.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.810
So people may donate pieces
to the Laura Ashley archive,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.840
that could be a Laura Ashley bowl

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.930
that you used to have in
your house in the 1980s

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.310
or your mum's wedding
dress from the 1970s.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.820
It's fantastic for us to
be able to see

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.140
that we're following in the footsteps
of the people who came before us.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:04.990
- We've got over three and a half million

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:08.800
of items under storage, be it
a box or be it an artefact.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.200
We have more than 40 rooms

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.880
of the same standard
or even better standard

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.470
than what I'm actually standing in now.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.700
This is probably one of the largest
single repositories in the country.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.360
Currently, we occupy 14 to 15%

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.830
of the actual mined void area underground,

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:26.230
which means we've got amazing
amounts of space to expand into.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:30.330
I mean, really, if you were
to convert this entire site,

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.700
eventually you'd probably be able to store

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.063
just about every single
archive box in Europe.

